---
/**
 * The rate card, or the honest absence of one.
 *
 * `hub.rates` is unset until there are numbers worth standing behind. Until
 * then, the page invites a founding-partnership conversation instead of
 * publishing a provisional price or a table with blanks in it.
 *
 * Audience figures are the same bet in reverse, in three states rather than
 * two: nothing counted, counting but too new to mean anything, and enough
 * weeks to quote. The middle state exists because switching analytics on
 * does not hand you an audience, and a page that starts advertising one the
 * same afternoon is making a claim it cannot support. Quoting a number
 * nobody counted is how a media kit stops being believed, and this whole
 * model runs on being believed.
 */
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaBand from '@/components/CtaBand.astro';
import { getHub, liveTowns } from '@/config';
import { formatDate, parseLocal, startOfDay } from '@/lib/dates';

/**
 * Thirty days before the page will talk about an audience at all. A week of
 * traffic is weather, not a number a buyer can plan against, and the first
 * days after launch are mostly us reloading our own pages.
 */
const MIN_DAYS = 30;

const hub = getHub();
const live = liveTowns();
const rates = hub.rates ?? [];
// The start date is part of what makes this claim true, not decoration: with
// a counter but no date, the page falls back to saying nothing is measured
// yet, which is the one wording that cannot be wrong. The validator errors on
// that combination so it does not survive a build unnoticed.
const since = hub.analyticsSince ? parseLocal(hub.analyticsSince) : null;
const counting = (Boolean(hub.ga4Id) || hub.analytics === true) && since !== null;
// Both ends are Denver midnights, so the gap is a whole number of days give
// or take the hour a DST change moves; rounding lands on the right day.
const days = since ? Math.round((startOfDay().getTime() - since.getTime()) / 86_400_000) : 0;
const measured = counting && days >= MIN_DAYS;
// "0 days ago" is the sort of sentence that tells a reader nobody is looking
// at this page.
const started =
  days <= 0
    ? 'We started counting today'
    : days === 1
      ? 'We started counting yesterday'
      : `We started counting on ${formatDate(since!)}, ${days} days ago`;
const contactHref = `/contact/?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Partnership enquiry')}#send-message`;
const money = (n: number) => `$${n.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;
---
<Page
  title="Advertise & partner"
  eyebrow="Work with us"
  description={`Reach residents and visitors across ${live.length} Colorado Front Range towns through Inside the Towns.`}
  intro={`Guides to ${live.length} towns that people read when they are deciding where to go this weekend, or whether to move here at all.`}
  narrow
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Inside the Towns reaches people who live in, visit, or are about to move to the small towns of the northern
      Front Range. Placements are clearly labeled and never mixed into the editorial listings.
    </p>

    <h2>What isn’t for sale</h2>
    <p>
      A place in the listings. Every guide tells its readers that nobody pays to be listed and nobody can pay to be
      ranked higher, and that promise is worth more to both of us than a featured slot would be — it is the reason
      anyone believes the listings in the first place. Sponsorship sits beside the editorial, labeled, never inside
      it. This is not a policy we will quietly change later; it is written into the
      <a href="/editorial/">editorial policy</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  {rates.length > 0 ? (
    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="display-md mb-6 text-3xl">What a placement costs</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="compare w-full text-left">
          <caption class="sr-only">Advertising rates</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Placement</th>
              <th scope="col">Where it appears</th>
              <th scope="col">Price</th>
              <th scope="col">How many</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {rates.map((rate) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" data-label="Placement">{rate.name}</th>
                <td data-label="Where it appears">
                  {rate.placement}
                  {rate.note && <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-ink-muted">{rate.note}</span>}
                </td>
                <td data-label="Price" class="whitespace-nowrap tabular-nums">
                  {money(rate.price)} <span class="text-ink-faint">/ {rate.period}</span>
                </td>
                <td data-label="How many">{rate.slots ? `${rate.slots} per town` : 'Ask'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[68ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        We keep placements to a small number per town so they do not compete with each other, which is also why the
        right-hand column is a real limit rather than a sales line.
      </p>
    </section>
  ) : (
    <div class="prose mt-14">
      <h2>Discuss a founding partnership</h2>
      <p>
        Interested in reaching local residents? <a href={contactHref}>Contact us to discuss a founding partnership.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  )}

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What’s available</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A labeled “presented by” slot on a town’s events page — the page people come back to weekly.</li>
      <li>A sponsor block on a town’s Moving Here page, which is where agents, lenders and movers are looked for.</li>
      <li>
        Network-wide sponsorship across {live.length === 1 ? 'the live guide' : `all ${live.length} live guides`}, for
        anyone serving the whole corridor rather than one town.
      </li>
      {hub.newsletter && <li>A labeled slot in the weekly email, beside the listings and never inside them.</li>}
    </ul>

    <h2>Audience</h2>
    {measured ? (
      <p>
        Current figures are in the media kit, sent on request: readers per town, email subscribers, and what people
        actually open, counted since {formatDate(since!)}. The numbers are what we measured, not what we hope.
      </p>
    ) : counting ? (
      <p>
        {started} — not long enough to tell you anything you should act on. Ask and you will get exactly what the
        counter says, with that start date attached, and you can decide for yourself whether it is worth anything
        yet. What you will not get is a projection. We would rather you came back in a month than bought on a number
        we had to squint at.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        We are not going to quote an audience number we have not measured. Traffic reporting is being set up now, and
        the moment there are real figures they go here and in the media kit — per town, dated, and honest about which
        towns are small. If you would rather wait for numbers before talking, that is the right instinct, and we will
        come to you when they exist.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={contactHref}>Send a partnership enquiry</a> and say which town or towns you care about. What suits a business
      serving the whole corridor is different from what suits one café, and knowing which you are saves us both a
      round of email.
    </p>
  </div>

  <CtaBand
    eyebrow="Before you spend anything"
    title="Look at what you would be next to"
    body="The guides are the pitch. Read a town's events page and its Moving Here page, and decide whether the company is one you want to keep."
    links={[
      { label: 'This weekend', href: '/this-weekend/', primary: true },
      { label: 'Moving here', href: '/moving/' },
    ]}
  />
</Page>
